I used a pointer named `nextNonZero` to mark where the next non-zero value should go. When a non-zero value is found, it is swapped into that position. This keeps the non-zero values in their original order, and the zeros naturally move behind them.

The array is changed in place. Here is a small dry run for `[0, 4, 0, 2]`: `4` goes to index `0`, then `2` goes to index `1`, giving `[4, 2, 0, 0]`.

```python
class Solution:
    def moveZeroes(self, nums):
        next_non_zero = 0

        for i in range(len(nums)):
            if nums[i] != 0:
                nums[next_non_zero], nums[i] = nums[i], nums[next_non_zero]
                next_non_zero += 1
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. An empty list and a list containing only zeros need no special handling.

I used a temporary slice to collect all non-zero values first. Then the original slice is rewritten with those values followed by zeros. This is easy to read and makes the order rule clear, since values are added in their original scan order.

The input slice is modified through `copy`. A dry run with `[5, 0, 8, 0, 1]` first collects `[5, 8, 1]`, then writes `[5, 8, 1, 0, 0]` back into `nums`.

```go
func moveZeroes(nums []int) {
    nonZeroValues := make([]int, 0, len(nums))

    for _, value := range nums {
        if value != 0 {
            nonZeroValues = append(nonZeroValues, value)
        }
    }

    copy(nums, nonZeroValues)

    for i := len(nonZeroValues); i < len(nums); i++ {
        nums[i] = 0
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the temporary slice. This extra memory is the trade-off for keeping the steps very direct.

The code below moves each non-zero value left by filling the current zero position. After all non-zero values have been placed, the remaining positions are filled with zeros.

For example, with `[0, 7, 0, 3]`, the first pass writes `7` and then `3`, so the front becomes `[7, 3, ...]`. The second pass completes it as `[7, 3, 0, 0]`. The order of `7` and `3` is kept.

This updates the given array instead of returning a new one.

```java
class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {
        int writeIndex = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value != 0) {
                nums[writeIndex] = value;
                writeIndex++;
            }
        }

        while (writeIndex < nums.length) {
            nums[writeIndex] = 0;
            writeIndex++;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. An empty array also works because both loops simply do nothing.

Here `stable_partition` puts all non-zero elements before the zeros while preserving their order. The returned iterator points to the first position that belongs to the zero section, so the rest of the array can be filled with `0`.

This is another in-place result, but the library may use extra working space internally. The important edge cases, such as an empty vector, all zeros, or no zeros, are handled by the same code.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {
        auto firstZero = stable_partition(
            nums.begin(),
            nums.end(),
            [](int value) {
                return value != 0;
            }
        );

        fill(firstZero, nums.end(), 0);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` in the general case for `stable_partition` when enough temporary storage is not available.
Space complexity: up to `O(n)` internal space, depending on the library implementation.

This version scans from left to right and keeps `writeIndex` at the first position that should receive a non-zero value. Each non-zero element is swapped into that position. Swapping is safe here because the values between the two indexes are zeros.

It changes `nums` directly and does not create another array. If the input already has no zeros, every swap is between an element and itself, so the result stays unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int writeIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] != 0) {
                swap(nums[writeIndex], nums[i]);
                writeIndex++;
            }
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the array is scanned once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

